EXT. DUSTY CROSSROADS GENERAL STORE - NOON

A false-fronted store beside a dry well. A hitching post. A sleeping dog under a porch. Heat shimmers off the road.

SHERIFF AMOS BRIDGEWATER (50s, sweat-stained hat) sits on the porch whittling. Beside him in a rocker, his deputy, TAMSIN FOURKILLER (30s, braid over her shoulder), sharpens a knife. Inside the doorway, the shopkeeper, WENDELL PRATT (60s, apron), sweeps with a straw broom that moves less than it should.

AMOS
Quiet day.

TAMSIN
Quiet town.

WENDELL
Quiet's good for business.

AMOS
Is it?

WENDELL
It's never been bad.

The sound of hooves. All three look up. A lone rider approaches from the south, dust rising behind him. He reins up at the well, and dismounts, slowly. HOLLIS CARDWELL (40s, gray duster, face like leather, a revolver on each hip) dips a tin cup in the well. He drinks, and watches them over the rim.

AMOS
Morning, stranger.

HOLLIS
Afternoon, I think.

AMOS
Might be.

Hollis wipes his mouth on his sleeve. Tamsin's knife stops against the whetstone. Amos does not stop whittling.

HOLLIS
Looking for a man.

AMOS
This town's got a few.

HOLLIS
This one's called Baxter.

Wendell's broom freezes. Amos blows a shaving off his blade.

AMOS
Don't know him.

HOLLIS
Funny. He knows you, Sheriff.

Nobody moves. The dog under the porch lifts its head, then lowers it.

TAMSIN
Mister, you picked a hot day.

HOLLIS
I picked this one on purpose.

AMOS
So what's your business?

HOLLIS
Same as yours, I expect. Old debts.

Amos closes his knife, slips it into his pocket, and stands. Hollis's hands hang loose at his sides.

AMOS
Wendell, go inside.

WENDELL
Sheriff --

AMOS
Inside.

Wendell backs through the door and shuts it. A bolt slides. Tamsin rises from the rocker, one hand on her hip.

HOLLIS
Deputy. You too.

TAMSIN
I'm fine here.

HOLLIS
Suit yourself.

A fly drones. Somewhere, a shutter bangs once in the wind.
